45
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
He says: as a Wolfden leader, I've been trying to figure out

46
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
what age-appropriate youth leadership means for eight-year-olds.

47
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
I got an education at our den meeting this week.

48
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,000
We're working on a belt loop requiring the wolves to create a project out of recycled material.

49
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
I didn't tell them how big the projects were to be or what shape or form they should take

50
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
or what would be good enough to satisfy the requirement,

51
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
And so they showed up with a bunch of stuff to build the project

52
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
and for about five minutes they floundered around.

53
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Some of the parents were looking at me to step in and provide guidance,

54
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
but I held back and observed just enough to ensure they were being safe and kind to one another.

55
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
And suddenly these six eight-year-old Cub Scouts

56
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,000
self-selected into two groups and things were taking shape.

57
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
One group built a table complete with storage space and a trash can,

58
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
and the other put together a castle complete with a moat

59
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
and a flag made of newspaper that we were all asked to salute. by the way,

60
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
They had more fun at this meeting than any of the other ones they've been at this year

61
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
and they were rightfully proud of what they created in 30 short minutes.

62
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
My eyes were opened, letting Cub's lead themselves

63
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
and create, with a few guidelines and some safety supervision had worked.

64
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Now, how often do we get frustrated when we see 12-year-old Scouts

65
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
mill around and apparently not accomplish anything at a troop meeting

66
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
and then we step in and try to help them?

67
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
We're robbing them if we fail to help them think through the objectives on their own ahead of time

68
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,000
and then try to step in and fix things that really aren't broken.

69
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Now I'm full of ideas to help my eight-year-old Cub's

70
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
continue to explore their creativity.

71
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
I just wanted to share this moment of epiphany that I finally understood

72
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
after six years as a Cub Scout leader

73
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
and three years as a Scouter with a troop.

74
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Oh, we've all had those moments and if you haven't had those there to come,

75
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,000
If you give it a shot, you know, if you step back, observe, let things happen.

76
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Don't try to fix things that aren't broken, because we're working with Scouts,

77
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
You know whether they're eight years old or 12 years old or 17 years old,

78
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,000
they have to sort things out and once you give them the room to do that

79
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
well, there are some pretty amazing results actually.

